Q2 – When Is ArrayList an Appropriate Engineering Choice?

1. Introduction
A music-streaming application uses a playlist to store songs. Users can add songs, insert songs at specific positions, remove songs, access a particular song and iterate through the playlist.

Java provides different collection classes, such as ArrayList, LinkedList and ArrayDeque, to perform these operations.
- ArrayList: Uses a dynamic array to store elements. It provides fast index-based access.
- LinkedList: Uses nodes connected through links. Insertion and deletion are efficient when the required position is already reached.
- ArrayDeque: Uses a resizable circular array. It is suitable for adding and removing elements at both ends.
2. Experimental Design
I would compare ArrayList and LinkedList using three different playlist sizes.
Experiment	Number of songs	Purpose
Small playlist	100	Test basic operations.
Medium playlist	10,000	Compare normal playlist usage.
Large playlist	100,000	Test performance under heavy usage.

The following operations will be tested:
1. Adding songs at the end.
2. Inserting songs at the front.
3. Inserting songs in the middle.
4. Removing songs from the front and middle.
5. Accessing the k-th song.
6. Iterating through the complete playlist.
Prediction:
- ArrayList should perform better for accessing songs by index.
- LinkedList should perform better for insertion and deletion when an iterator is already at the required position.
- ArrayList should generally perform better for iteration because of better memory locality.
3. Java Implementation
The following program compares ArrayList and LinkedList for different playlist sizes and operations.

import java.util.*;

public class PlaylistComparison {

    static volatile int result;

    static void test(int size) {

        ArrayList<Integer> arrayList = new ArrayList<>();
        LinkedList<Integer> linkedList = new LinkedList<>();

        for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {
            arrayList.add(i);
            linkedList.add(i);
        }

        System.out.println("\nPlaylist Size: " + size);

        // Add at end
        long start = System.nanoTime();

        arrayList.add(size);

        long end = System.nanoTime();

        System.out.println("ArrayList Add End: "
            + (end - start) + " ns");

        start = System.nanoTime();

        linkedList.add(size);

        end = System.nanoTime();

        System.out.println("LinkedList Add End: "
            + (end - start) + " ns");

        // Insert at front
        start = System.nanoTime();

        arrayList.add(0, -1);

        end = System.nanoTime();

        System.out.println("ArrayList Insert Front: "
            + (end - start) + " ns");

        start = System.nanoTime();

        linkedList.add(0, -1);

        end = System.nanoTime();

        System.out.println("LinkedList Insert Front: "
            + (end - start) + " ns");

        // Insert in middle
        int middle = size / 2;

        start = System.nanoTime();

        arrayList.add(middle, -2);

        end = System.nanoTime();

        System.out.println("ArrayList Insert Middle: "
            + (end - start) + " ns");

        start = System.nanoTime();

        linkedList.add(middle, -2);

        end = System.nanoTime();

        System.out.println("LinkedList Insert Middle: "
            + (end - start) + " ns");

        // Remove from front
        start = System.nanoTime();

        arrayList.remove(0);

        end = System.nanoTime();

        System.out.println("ArrayList Remove Front: "
            + (end - start) + " ns");

        start = System.nanoTime();

        linkedList.remove(0);

        end = System.nanoTime();

        System.out.println("LinkedList Remove Front: "
            + (end - start) + " ns");

        // Remove from middle
        start = System.nanoTime();

        arrayList.remove(middle);

        end = System.nanoTime();

        System.out.println("ArrayList Remove Middle: "
            + (end - start) + " ns");

        start = System.nanoTime();

        linkedList.remove(middle);

        end = System.nanoTime();

        System.out.println("LinkedList Remove Middle: "
            + (end - start) + " ns");

        // Access k-th element
        start = System.nanoTime();

        result = arrayList.get(size / 2);

        end = System.nanoTime();

        System.out.println("ArrayList Get Kth: "
            + (end - start) + " ns");

        start = System.nanoTime();

        result = linkedList.get(size / 2);

        end = System.nanoTime();

        System.out.println("LinkedList Get Kth: "
            + (end - start) + " ns");

        // Iterate through list
        start = System.nanoTime();

        int sum = 0;

        for (int value : arrayList) {
            sum += value;
        }

        result = sum;

        end = System.nanoTime();

        System.out.println("ArrayList Iteration: "
            + (end - start) + " ns");

        start = System.nanoTime();

        sum = 0;

        for (int value : linkedList) {
            sum += value;
        }

        result = sum;

        end = System.nanoTime();

        System.out.println("LinkedList Iteration: "
            + (end - start) + " ns");
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        test(100);

        test(10000);

        test(100000);
    }
}



4. Expected Experimental Results
The following table shows the expected performance trends, not actual measured timings.
Operation	ArrayList	LinkedList
Add at end	Very fast, amortized	Very fast
Insert at front	Slow	Fast
Insert in middle	Slow	Requires traversal
Remove at front	Slow	Fast
Remove in middle	Slow	Requires traversal
Access k-th element	Very fast	Slow
Full iteration	Generally fast	Generally slower

Explanation:
- ArrayList shifts elements when inserting or removing at the front or middle.
- LinkedList does not need to shift elements, but must traverse nodes to reach a particular position.
- ArrayList supports direct index access in constant time.
- LinkedList requires sequential traversal to access an indexed element.
5. Analysis of Results
After executing the program, compare the measured timings for each playlist size.

The expected observations are:
1. Adding at the end: Both ArrayList and LinkedList provide efficient insertion at the end.
2. Inserting at the front: LinkedList is faster because it only updates node links, whereas ArrayList shifts existing elements.
3. Inserting in the middle: LinkedList must traverse the list to reach the position. ArrayList must shift elements after the insertion position.
4. Removing elements: LinkedList is efficient when the required node is already reached. ArrayList must shift elements after removal.
5. Accessing the k-th song: ArrayList is much faster because it directly accesses an index. LinkedList traverses nodes.
6. Iteration: ArrayList generally performs better because its elements are stored in a continuous array.
When Is ArrayList a Reasonable Engineering Choice?
ArrayList is a reasonable choice when:
- Users frequently access songs by index.
- Songs are mostly added at the end.
- Users frequently browse or iterate through playlists.
- Insertions and deletions at the front or middle are relatively uncommon.
I would stop recommending ArrayList when frequent insertions and deletions at the beginning or middle become the dominant operations.
6. Changed Condition – Up Next Queue
The application introduces an Up Next queue where songs are frequently added at both ends and removed from the front.

In this situation, I recommend ArrayDeque instead of ArrayList.
Reasons:
- ArrayDeque supports insertion at both ends.
- Removing songs from the front is efficient.
- It does not require shifting all remaining elements.
- It uses a resizable circular array.
- It is generally more suitable for queue operations than ArrayList.
Example Java implementation:
import java.util.ArrayDeque;

public class UpNextQueue {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        ArrayDeque<String> queue = new ArrayDeque<>();

        // Add songs at the end
        queue.addLast("Song A");
        queue.addLast("Song B");

        // Add a song at the front
        queue.addFirst("Song C");

        // Remove the first song
        queue.removeFirst();

        // Display the queue
        System.out.println(queue);
    }
}
Expected output:
[Song A, Song B]
Therefore, ArrayDeque is a better choice for the Up Next queue, while ArrayList remains suitable for the normal playlist.
7. Alternative Concept and Complexity Analysis
The following table compares the time complexity of ArrayList, LinkedList and ArrayDeque.
Operation	ArrayList	LinkedList	ArrayDeque
Add at end	O(1) amortized	O(1)	O(1) amortized
Insert at front	O(n)	O(1)	O(1) amortized
Insert in middle	O(n)	O(n)	O(n)
Get k-th element	O(1)	O(n)	O(n)
Iterate entire list	O(n)	O(n)	O(n)
Remove from front	O(n)	O(1)	O(1) amortized
Remove from middle	O(n)	O(n)	O(n)

Note: LinkedList insertion and deletion take O(1) once the required node is reached using an iterator. Finding that position takes O(n). ArrayDeque does not support direct indexed insertion or access.
Space Complexity
Data structure	Space used per element
ArrayList	One array reference slot per element
LinkedList	One node containing the element reference and previous/next links
ArrayDeque	One array reference slot per element

ArrayList and ArrayDeque generally use less memory per element than LinkedList because LinkedList requires additional node and link information.
Alternative Concept – Plain Array
If the playlist were stored in a normal array, we would have to manually shift elements when inserting or removing songs.

Example:
// Inserting a song at the front
for (int i = size; i > 0; i--) {
    songs[i] = songs[i - 1];
}

songs[0] = newSong;
size++;
To remove a song from the front:
for (int i = 0; i < size - 1; i++) {
    songs[i] = songs[i + 1];
}

size--;
Complexity comparison:
- Accessing the k-th song: O(1).
- Adding at the end: O(1) if space is available.
- Inserting at the front or middle: O(n).
- Removing at the front or middle: O(n).
- Iterating through all songs: O(n).
A plain array has fixed capacity, so increasing its size requires creating a new array and copying the elements.
